# API Schema Validation Rules

## Purpose

These rules define how API response schemas and runtime contract validation should be implemented in the Playwright + TypeScript test automation framework.

The goals are:

- reduce repetitive manual response type assertions;
- keep API specs readable;
- separate schema validation from behavior assertions;
- provide reusable runtime validation for non-trivial API contracts;
- avoid duplicated TypeScript types and runtime validation logic;
- keep Zod usage consistent across API tests.

---

## Core Principle

Use Zod schema validation for API response shape.

Use Playwright `expect` for API behavior.

Zod schemas validate structure and types.

Assertion helpers connect Zod validation to Playwright assertions.

Specs should remain focused on:

- request;
- status code;
- behavior;
- business expectations;
- contract-relevant assertions.

---

## Responsibility Split

### Zod Schema Owns

Zod schemas should validate API response structure:

- object shape;
- required fields;
- optional fields;
- nullable fields;
- primitive types;
- enum values;
- nested objects;
- arrays;
- pagination envelopes;
- reusable contract models.

### Shared Zod Assertion Helper Owns

The shared Zod assertion helper should:

- call `schema.safeParse`;
- convert schema validation failures into readable Playwright assertion failures;
- return parsed typed data;
- contain reusable schema-validation plumbing only.

### Feature-Specific Assertion Helper Owns

Feature-specific API assertion helpers should:

- expose domain-readable assertion names;
- call the shared Zod assertion helper;
- return parsed typed data when useful;
- contain reusable behavior assertions only when those assertions belong to the feature.

### Spec Owns

API specs should:

- send the request;
- assert status code;
- call feature-specific assertion helpers;
- assert scenario-specific behavior;
- keep test intent visible.

---

## When To Use Zod

Use Zod schemas when API responses are:

- reused across multiple tests;
- nested;
- paginated;
- contract-critical;
- used by multiple assertion helpers;
- large enough that manual type checks reduce readability;
- shared across API tests and UI setup checks;
- likely to evolve and benefit from centralized schema ownership.

Good candidates:

- login response;
- registration response;
- product response;
- paginated product list;
- contact message response;
- user profile response;
- order response;
- cart response.

---

## When Not To Use Zod

Do not create Zod schemas for every trivial one-off response.

Manual assertions are acceptable for small one-off responses when:

- the body has one or two fields;
- the shape is not reused;
- schema extraction would add more noise than value;
- the response is not contract-critical;
- behavior assertion is more important than reusable shape validation.

Example where manual assertion may be enough:

    expect(body.success).toBe(true);

---

## Schema Location

Reusable API schemas belong in `src/test/schemas/api/`.

Examples:

- `src/test/schemas/api/contact.schema.ts`
- `src/test/schemas/api/product.schema.ts`
- `src/test/schemas/api/pagination.schema.ts`
- `src/test/schemas/api/user.schema.ts`

Do not define reusable Zod schemas inside specs.

Do not define API schemas in:

- Page Objects;
- Component Objects;
- builders;
- generators;
- API clients;
- fixtures;
- domain flows.

---

## Assertion Helper Location

API assertion helpers belong in `src/test/assertions/api/`.

Examples:

- `src/test/assertions/api/contact-response.assertion.ts`
- `src/test/assertions/api/product-response.assertion.ts`
- `src/test/assertions/api/login-response.assertion.ts`

Assertion helpers may import schemas from `src/test/schemas/api/`.

Specs should import feature-specific assertion helpers, not raw schemas, when a reusable assertion helper exists.

---

## Shared Zod Assertion Helper

Use a shared Zod assertion helper for the repeated `safeParse` plus Playwright `expect` pattern.

The shared helper should live in `src/test/assertions/api/`.

Recommended file name: `src/test/assertions/api/zod-schema.assertion.ts`.

The shared helper should:

- accept a Zod schema;
- accept an unknown value;
- call `schema.safeParse`;
- fail through Playwright `expect` with useful Zod error details;
- return parsed typed data.

Feature-specific API assertion helpers should import the shared Zod assertion helper.

Do not duplicate the same `safeParse` plus `expect` wrapper logic in every response assertion helper.

Specs should normally import feature-specific assertion helpers instead of raw schemas or the generic Zod helper.

Good spec usage:

    const body = expectContactSendMessageResponse(await response.json());

Avoid this in specs when a feature-specific helper exists:

    const body = expectToMatchSchema(
      contactSendMessageResponseSchema,
      await response.json(),
    );

The shared Zod assertion helper must not contain behavior assertions.

Behavior assertions belong in specs or dedicated behavior assertion helpers.

Good responsibility split:

- Zod schema validates response shape;
- shared Zod assertion helper owns `safeParse` plus Playwright `expect`;
- feature-specific response helper owns domain-readable assertion name;
- spec owns request, status code, and behavior assertions.

Feature-specific response helpers should stay thin unless they also own reusable behavior assertions.

Example responsibility:

- `expectContactSendMessageResponse` validates Contact response shape through the shared helper;
- `expectPaginatedProductSortingResponse` validates Product response shape through the shared helper;
- `expectProductsSorted` validates sorting behavior separately.

Do not call the shared Zod assertion helper directly from specs when a feature-specific helper exists.

Do not put behavior checks into the shared Zod assertion helper.

Do not put response-specific field expectations into the shared Zod assertion helper.

Do not put API request logic into the shared Zod assertion helper.

---

## Preferred Zod Schema Pattern

Use named exported schemas.

Infer TypeScript types from schemas.

Preferred shape:

    import { z } from 'zod';

    export const contactSendMessageResponseSchema = z.object({
      id: z.string().min(1),
      name: z.string(),
      email: z.string(),
      subject: z.string(),
      message: z.string(),
      status: z.string().min(1),
      created_at: z.string().min(1),
    });

    export type ContactSendMessageResponse = z.infer<
      typeof contactSendMessageResponseSchema
    >;

Avoid maintaining a separate manual interface for the same response shape unless there is a clear reason.

Bad shape:

    type ContactSendMessageResponse = {
      id: string;
      name: string;
    };

    export const contactSendMessageResponseSchema = z.object({
      id: z.string(),
      name: z.string(),
    });

This duplicates type ownership.

---

## Preferred Assertion Helper Pattern

Use feature-specific assertion helpers to expose readable domain names.

Feature-specific assertion helpers should normally delegate schema validation to the shared Zod assertion helper.

Preferred shape:

    import {
      contactSendMessageResponseSchema,
      type ContactSendMessageResponse,
    } from '../../schemas/api/contact.schema';
    import { expectToMatchSchema } from './zod-schema.assertion';

    export function expectContactSendMessageResponse(
      body: unknown,
    ): ContactSendMessageResponse {
      return expectToMatchSchema(
        contactSendMessageResponseSchema,
        body,
        'Contact send-message response schema validation failed',
      );
    }

Specs can then use the typed parsed value:

    const body = expectContactSendMessageResponse(await response.json());

    expect(body.status).toBe('NEW');

---

## Schema Validation vs Behavior Assertions

Do not hide behavior assertions inside Zod schemas.

Zod schemas should not validate behavior such as:

- sorted order;
- filtered result predicates;
- token usability;
- authorization workflow;
- business process outcomes;
- navigation outcomes;
- visual state.

Behavior assertions belong in specs or dedicated assertion helpers.

Good split:

    const body = expectPaginatedProductsResponse(await response.json());

    expectProductsSorted(body.data, 'price', 'asc');

Here:

- `expectPaginatedProductsResponse` validates response shape;
- `expectProductsSorted` validates behavior.

---

## Avoid Duplicate Contract Assertions

When a non-trivial response is already validated through a feature-specific Zod assertion helper, treat that helper call as the primary contract assertion.

Do not repeat large field-by-field manual assertions that only restate the same response shape already enforced by Zod.

After schema validation, keep manual assertions focused on scenario behavior invariants, for example:

- response identity or correlation with request data that expresses behavior under test;
- security behavior such as omission of sensitive fields;
- business-state expectations not modeled as pure structure.

If a manual assertion adds no behavior value beyond Zod shape validation, remove it.

---

## Pagination Schemas

Reusable pagination envelopes should be modeled once when multiple endpoints use the same structure.

Preferred shape:

    import { z } from 'zod';

    export function paginatedResponseSchema<TItem extends z.ZodTypeAny>(
      itemSchema: TItem,
    ) {
      return z.object({
        current_page: z.number(),
        data: z.array(itemSchema),
        per_page: z.number(),
        total: z.number(),
      });
    }

Use generic pagination schemas only when they reduce duplication.

Do not introduce complex schema factories for one-off responses.

---

## Enum And Literal Values

Use Zod enums or literal unions for documented enum values.

Preferred shape:

    export const contactStatusSchema = z.enum([
      'NEW',
      'ON_HOLD',
      'IN_PROGRESS',
      'RESOLVED',
    ]);

Do not assert undocumented enum values.

If live behavior differs from the contract, document the contract gap instead of silently widening the schema.

---

## Optional And Nullable Fields

Schema optionality must match the API contract.

Use `z.string().optional()` only when the field may be omitted.

Use `z.string().nullable()` only when the field may be present with `null`.

Use `z.string().nullish()` only when both omitted and `null` are valid.

Do not mark fields optional or nullable only to make failing tests pass.

If the contract is unclear, report a contract gap.

---

## Strictness Policy

Schema strictness must match the API contract.

Use `.strict()` only when the contract explicitly forbids additional fields.

Prefer non-strict object schemas when the API may return additional fields.

Do not fail tests because of harmless undocumented additional fields unless strict shape is part of the contract.

Do not assert undocumented fields unless they are required by the behavior under test.

---

## Date And Timestamp Fields

For timestamp fields, prefer validating that the value is a non-empty string unless the contract explicitly defines a date format.

Example:

    created_at: z.string().min(1)

Use stricter datetime validation only when the API contract requires it.

Example:

    created_at: z.string().datetime()

Do not guess timestamp format requirements.

---

## API Client Rule

API clients must not contain schema assertions.

API clients may:

- compose requests;
- send requests;
- return responses or parsed JSON only if that is the project convention.

API clients must not:

- call Playwright `expect`;
- call Zod schema validation as an assertion;
- hide behavior checks.

Schema validation belongs in assertion helpers or specs.

---

## Spec Usage Rule

Specs should not call `schema.parse()` directly when a reusable assertion helper exists.

Prefer:

    const body = expectContactSendMessageResponse(await response.json());

Avoid:

    const body = contactSendMessageResponseSchema.parse(await response.json());

Reason:

- direct `parse()` throws Zod errors instead of Playwright assertion failures;
- assertion helpers provide consistent reporting;
- helpers keep spec intent clearer.

Direct schema usage in specs is acceptable only for very small local experiments or when no reusable helper exists yet.

---

## Error Reporting

Schema validation failures should produce useful output.

Assertion helpers should include Zod error details in the Playwright assertion message when practical.

Preferred shared helper behavior:

- format failing Zod issues;
- include field path and message;
- fail through Playwright `expect`;
- return parsed data on success.

Do not swallow schema errors.

Do not replace schema errors with vague messages such as `Invalid response` unless detailed schema output is also available.

---

## Migration Policy

Do not migrate every existing API helper to Zod in one large change.

Introduce Zod incrementally.

Good migration candidates:

- response helpers with many manual type checks;
- paginated responses;
- nested responses;
- response shapes reused across multiple specs.

Avoid mass rewrites that mix:

- schema migration;
- new test coverage;
- API client refactor;
- builder refactor;
- unrelated cleanup.

Preferred migration flow:

1. Add schema for one response family.
2. Update assertion helper to use the shared Zod assertion helper.
3. Keep existing behavior assertions unchanged.
4. Run impacted API specs and quality gate.
5. Review.

---

## Dependency Policy

Do not add `zod` dependency silently.

If `zod` is not already installed:

- propose adding it explicitly;
- explain why it is justified;
- keep the initial migration small;
- update package files only as part of an approved dependency change.

Do not add alternative schema libraries unless explicitly requested.

---

## Anti-Patterns

Avoid:

- large manual `typeof` assertion blocks for reusable response shapes;
- Zod schemas inside specs;
- Zod schemas inside Page Objects or Components;
- Zod schemas inside builders or generators;
- API clients that perform assertions;
- behavior checks hidden inside schemas;
- schema validation that guesses undocumented fields;
- `.strict()` without contract support;
- separate TypeScript interfaces duplicating Zod-inferred types;
- migrating all API assertions at once;
- creating schemas for trivial one-off responses;
- duplicating `safeParse` plus `expect` wrapper logic in every assertion helper;
- calling the shared Zod assertion helper directly from specs when a feature-specific helper exists.
- large field-by-field manual assertions that duplicate contract checks already covered by Zod.

---

## Main Principle

Schema validation proves response shape.

Behavior assertions prove API behavior.

The shared Zod assertion helper connects schema validation to Playwright reporting.

Feature-specific assertion helpers provide readable domain names.

Specs stay focused on readable API scenarios.
